36,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"My findings are concerning. The late-turn pool is severely thinned, and roadway and signals crews are a third below establishment. However, the lift and escalator maintenance contract currently has spare capacity.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"The mid-city runs are taking way longer than they used to, but the crowds aren't the problem. Also, people are wasting time waiting for connections, not at the ticket machines. We need to fix the actual travel time.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"It appears we are facing a financial paradox. We have ring-fenced construction funds from a lapsed grant, yet our operating and technology budgets are being trimmed. Furthermore, ticketing fees are now outweighing our resurfacing budget.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Servas,,"History offers a glimmer of hope. A late-evening pilot once met its cost-recovery targets, and while some lane-priority projects failed, the lift and entrance rebuilds were completed successfully and on time.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My research reveals significant risks. Bidders are pricing in heavy contingencies for work in pre-war shells due to unrecorded voids. Additionally, ferry and rail offices have never agreed on common passenger data terms.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Takumi, if travel time is the issue, we must consider if we have the crew to support it. My data shows roadway and signals crews are severely understaffed, which could hinder surface improvements.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, that's a problem. If we can't staff the roads, we can't fix the mid-city delays. But we can't just ignore the fact that passengers are stuck waiting for connections. We need a plan that actually moves people.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, your mention of ""unrecorded voids"" in pre-war shells sounds like a financial nightmare waiting to happen. Given our trimmed technology budget, we should be wary of any plan requiring extensive structural work or complex new integrations.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Servas,,"It is heartening to see the pieces fitting together. If the late-evening pilot was successful and the lift rebuilds were reliable, we have proven paths we can trust without gambling on the unknown.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Benjamin, you are correct. The structural risks in the older stations are substantial and unpredictable. Furthermore, the lack of agreement on data terms between ferry and rail makes a unified ticketing system seem overly optimistic.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, your point about the lift rebuilds is interesting. Since I found that the lift and escalator maintenance contract has spare capacity, station access upgrades seem the most operationally viable.","{""words"":30,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Asuka, I agree that we need something viable, but we can't forget the mid-city delays. If we can't fix the roads, maybe we can at least fix how people move between platforms to reduce those connection wait times.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Asuka, @Takumi, your alignment is promising. If we utilize the ring-fenced construction funds for station upgrades, we avoid draining our trimmed operating budget while leveraging existing maintenance capacity. It is a logically sound path.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Asuka"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Servas,,"It is a pleasure to see such harmony. By choosing a path with a successful historical precedent and current operational capacity, we honor the past while securing the future. It seems we are converging on a wise choice.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I agree. Given the unpredictability of the pre-war shells and the bureaucratic deadlock between ferry and rail, the station upgrades appear to be the most stable and realistic option for us to pursue.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"It seems we have reached a consensus. Station access upgrades align with our available maintenance capacity, utilize construction funds, and avoid the risks associated with staffing shortages and structural voids. I am satisfied with this direction.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Takumi,,"I'm on board. If it helps people get between platforms faster and we actually have the crew to do it, it's the best way to cut down those wasted minutes. Let's go with it.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"It is a rare pleasure when logic and practicality align so seamlessly. By avoiding the pitfalls of operational deficits and structural uncertainty, we ensure a sustainable improvement. I am fully in agreement.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Servas,,It is a wonderful conclusion. We have listened to the warnings of the present and the lessons of the past to find a path of stability. I am happy to support this decision.,"{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
55,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I concur. By steering clear of the data disputes and the hidden dangers of the old shells, we choose the path of least resistance and greatest reliability. I am in full agreement.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
